 "captions": [
  {
   "from": 17,
   "to": 104,
   "text": "The word comes from the Latin \"draco\", a giant serpent."
  },
  {
   "from": 106,
   "to": 195,
   "text": "Around the year 600, Isidore of Seville wrote the encyclopedia"
  },
  {
   "from": 197,
   "to": 291,
   "text": "that medieval Europe trusted most after the Bible."
  },
  {
   "from": 293,
   "to": 371,
   "text": "His dragon was the largest creature on Earth."
  },
  {
   "from": 373,
   "to": 433,
   "text": "It had a crest and a small mouth,"
  },
  {
   "from": 435,
   "to": 503,
   "text": "and its power was not in its teeth."
  },
  {
   "from": 505,
   "to": 536,
   "text": "It was in its tail."
  },
  {
   "from": 537,
   "to": 625,
   "text": "The bestiaries, the illustrated animal books of the twelfth"
  },
  {
   "from": 627,
   "to": 671,
   "text": "and thirteenth centuries, added the details."
  },
  {
   "from": 673,
   "to": 763,
   "text": "A dragon waited on the paths elephants used, knotted its"
  },
  {
   "from": 764,
   "to": 878,
   "text": "tail around their legs, and squeezed until they suffocated."
  },
  {
   "from": 880,
   "to": 964,
   "text": "It feared one animal above all: the panther,"
  },
  {
   "from": 966,
   "to": 1039,
   "text": "whose sweet breath drew every creature to it,"
  },
  {
   "from": 1040,
   "to": 1116,
   "text": "except the dragon, which fled into hiding."
  },
  {
   "from": 1117,
   "to": 1202,
   "text": "And in these books, the dragon did not breathe fire."
  },
  {
   "from": 1204,
   "to": 1250,
   "text": "It didn't need to."
  }
 ],
